WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:11.660
- Okay, well in Rebecca's first slide

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:14.050
she presented what I was planning to say

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:18.280
which the issues that we
have are diverse locations,

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:21.700
heterogeneous data and
lack of software tools.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:24.263
But since I have half an
hour I'll say it again so.

00:00:26.574 --> 00:00:29.070
And first of all by background,

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:31.090
I wouldn't say I'm a
trained computer scientist

00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:32.410
but we can get into that.

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:34.250
But I'm a computer scientist

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:38.400
and my view of computer
science is we are tool makers

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:43.380
and so my goal here is like
what tools do you need.

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:46.340
I think Rebecca referenced you know

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:48.480
continually looking for available tools.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.010
No no no, go out and
specify the tools you need

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:55.620
and that's what we live
for is new problems.

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:57.280
And I also want to make the point

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.140
about machine vision, machine learning.

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:00.610
Machines do not see.

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:01.980
Machines do not learn.

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:03.990
People do and we're making tools

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:06.390
to extend what people can see

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:08.780
and extend what people can learn.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:11.430
And what I wanted to talk about is what

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:13.430
I mean there's all this great promise,

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:17.250
I mean you're reading all the
stuff that AI and so forth

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:18.890
is gonna make happen.

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:21.024
Like what what's standing in the way?

00:01:21.024 --> 00:01:24.130
In my view is a lack of data.

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:28.040
Particularly, some kinds
of data not quantity

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:30.090
and the lack of tools.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:33.080
So, I just wanted to
present a few thoughts

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:35.000
along these lines

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:38.543
and I thought I'd start by
ripping from today's headlines.

00:01:40.979 --> 00:01:44.050
One long-term project that
I work on fits and starts

00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:46.270
is learning about stone weathering

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:48.020
from the point of view
of computer graphics,

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:50.790
how to make realistic models.

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:53.454
And from the point of
view of conservation,

00:01:53.454 --> 00:01:55.090
what are the mechanisms?

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:57.670
How can we avoid it?

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:02.150
And so one thought for
many years is you know

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:06.450
we can get pictures and
scans of objects today

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:09.327
and these are you know
sculptures from Notre Dame

00:02:09.327 --> 00:02:14.327
and we can see all of the
stone loss that there is

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:17.870
and we can estimate the
shapes and so forth of these.

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:20.030
And we can learn something

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:22.520
about the stone weathering process

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:25.220
by looking at historic
photos of the same things,

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:28.660
monuments of this sort people
have been taking photos of

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:31.920
but it turns out to be
really difficult to get

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:35.030
photos and reliable photos

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:37.730
even though there should be collections.

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:39.300
We can see from the ones we find

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:41.900
that there's been tremendous loss.

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:46.530
This is an image from a
postcard that was for sale.

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:48.870
So, unless I wanted to
cough up the money to buy it

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:51.460
I have no idea where this was taken

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:54.150
and when it was taken,
useful pieces of data

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:56.963
that you need if we wanna
conduct this kind of study.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:02:59.040
This one's really weird.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:01.590
It's from a poster that Walmart's selling

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:03.550
that is based on an old postcard.

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:05.821
So, it really have lost
where it came from.

00:03:05.821 --> 00:03:08.940
But what's interesting is
there's not as much loss here.

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:10.550
So, like where on the building is this?

00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:12.223
Why is there less loss?

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:15.140
And then like aha!

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:16.970
Here I have a picture that is

00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:18.520
from the Yale visual collection.

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:20.840
I must have all the data about that.

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:22.900
I can finally know like
where was this taken,

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:24.350
when was it taken.

00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:26.340
Well, it probably wasn't taken

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:30.176
in the published created
date of 1220 to 1240.

00:03:30.176 --> 00:03:34.640
So, I don't know you know
if there's no information

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:35.510
on the postcard.

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:37.640
If this was what was on the reverse

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:38.890
or if somebody just assumed

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:41.150
that when people looking
through the visual collection

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:43.060
they were interested in the Cathedral

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:45.120
rather than stone weathering

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:49.590
so all the sort of missing data

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:51.820
that either wasn't entered

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:55.720
or if somebody had it
now can't enter right now

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:57.763
because this is a fixed asset.

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:04.470
To a more successful project
from a colleague in France

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:07.580
who's studied Notre Dame a great deal.

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:10.860
He has studied these kings in front

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:11.842
which of course and the theme of

00:04:11.842 --> 00:04:14.177
we've been losing Notre
Dame for a long time

00:04:14.177 --> 00:04:16.010
these are not the original kings.

00:04:16.010 --> 00:04:19.040
The original kings were
removed and beheaded in the,

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:21.240
not kidding, they they took down the kings

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:24.530
the stone sculptures of
the kings from Notre Dame

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:28.630
and publicly beheaded them
in the French Revolution

00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:31.300
and only in 1977 the heads were found

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:33.910
in the back of somebody's house.

00:04:33.910 --> 00:04:38.330
Anyway, so what he did
was studied the heads

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:41.110
and and the remains in
the in the Cathedral

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:42.373
which they have also 3D scanned.

00:04:42.373 --> 00:04:45.620
There's been a lot of news
media about the guy at Vassar

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:48.540
and in Assassin's Creed
and other people who've

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:50.710
but you know obviously
the French have also.

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:55.710
Anyway, he examined remnants
of colors on the heads

00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:59.080
and on the structure.

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:00.650
So, bringing together this theme

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:04.130
of having to bring
together lots of sources

00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:05.480
just like you have lots of sources.

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:07.540
You have this purple flower
but there's all this stuff

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:09.450
you've got to bring together.

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:11.490
And he also brought together imagery

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:13.615
of how the light changes on the front

00:05:13.615 --> 00:05:16.420
of the cathedral at sunset.

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:20.590
And did some hypotheses
of what the cathedral

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:24.180
actually looked like in medieval times.

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:27.060
So, the point here being
you know that the learning

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:31.110
requires assembling
heterogeneous data sets

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:33.023
from many locations.

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:37.294
So, with those themes I
just wanted to talk about

00:05:37.294 --> 00:05:42.294
you know three aspects here using 3D, 2D

00:05:44.470 --> 00:05:47.460
and organizing collections.

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:49.780
And I realized I don't have the time here

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:52.480
so would you do this, okay.

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:57.480
Okay so, and in 3D, I mean so for you know

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:00.103
as far as computer vision for 30 years

00:06:00.103 --> 00:06:02.520
probably you say 40,

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:03.780
we've had all this research

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:08.460
on how to do active scanning
and and photogrammetry.

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:11.037
And you know these are
projects I've been involved in

00:06:11.037 --> 00:06:13.157
and of course there's been
thousands and thousands

00:06:13.157 --> 00:06:18.157
all over the world in capturing
3D of cultural heritag

00:06:18.637 --> 00:06:22.513
and natural history collections.

00:06:23.450 --> 00:06:26.400
And these are good for
some individual studies.

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:31.400
Like these are studies of
one of Michelangelo's Pietàs.

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:33.730
How was it meant to be viewed?

00:06:33.730 --> 00:06:35.436
What did it look like when Michelangelo

00:06:35.436 --> 00:06:39.230
broke it apart before it was repaired?

00:06:39.230 --> 00:06:42.090
These are from the
Egyptian Museum in Cairo.

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:45.190
How are these objects used?

00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:49.490
And here from a that we did at Yale

00:06:49.490 --> 00:06:53.420
a special exhibit that brought together

00:06:53.420 --> 00:06:57.070
many busts of the poet of Alexander Pope

00:06:57.070 --> 00:07:01.019
by the same artist Roubilliac

00:07:01.019 --> 00:07:03.910
scanning out these to compare

00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:06.743
how the representation changed over time.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:11.820
I should say the Smithsonian
has spectacular 3D scans

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:13.320
and particularly I was taken by this one.

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:15.970
This is enormously technically difficult

00:07:15.970 --> 00:07:19.170
must have been to challenge
and incredibly informative

00:07:19.170 --> 00:07:22.080
it's the the inside of Apollo 11

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:24.430
which you know I hopefully
everybody will visit this year

00:07:24.430 --> 00:07:26.540
and on the anniversary.

00:07:26.540 --> 00:07:28.730
And it's got great data about it

00:07:28.730 --> 00:07:31.420
including this little nugget
about astronaut graffiti

00:07:31.420 --> 00:07:33.000
that I wish they said more about.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:35.340
I'd like like buzzes what what did,

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:37.695
what do you mean astronaut graffiti?

00:07:37.695 --> 00:07:41.930
So, also I went yes there was a later talk

00:07:41.930 --> 00:07:44.370
is activating an artwork at the Hirshhorn.

00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:46.410
I don't know if people at the Hirshhorn

00:07:46.410 --> 00:07:48.521
realized that this sculptor by Ferren

00:07:48.521 --> 00:07:52.370
inspired a graduate
student named Cindy Gorl

00:07:52.370 --> 00:07:56.250
to start a whole line of
computer graphics research for

00:07:56.250 --> 00:07:58.030
well that goes on today in a forum

00:07:58.030 --> 00:08:00.930
in so called global illumination.

00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:05.220
And this is the Hirshhorns
official picture of it

00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:06.600
which is pretty good

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:09.876
but you know she had this
one taken to emphasize

00:08:09.876 --> 00:08:12.650
this lighting effect
that was the inspiration

00:08:12.650 --> 00:08:15.790
that and that so there was a model built

00:08:15.790 --> 00:08:17.920
to show this is in 1980 or so

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:21.410
how well we could've simulated
the lighting on this.

00:08:21.410 --> 00:08:23.510
And by the end of the eighties

00:08:23.510 --> 00:08:26.490
the improvements in lighting algorithms

00:08:26.490 --> 00:08:29.950
so another modeling little anecdote

00:08:29.950 --> 00:08:32.500
that's associated with the
work at the Smithsonian.

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:38.680
Anyway so there's a lot
of examples of scanning.

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:40.240
I mean like thousands and thousands

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:42.400
of scanning for documentation,

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:44.250
scanning for public communication

00:08:44.250 --> 00:08:47.071
but not that much what
have we really learned?

00:08:47.071 --> 00:08:50.370
What kind of analysis that we
do that gives us new insights

00:08:50.370 --> 00:08:51.930
into the past from these.

00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:54.330
So, a group of us got together to sort

00:08:54.330 --> 00:08:55.870
of review the literature and find out

00:08:55.870 --> 00:08:58.080
particularly with respect to geometry,

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:00.240
that's 3D scanning's about,

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:02.799
what have people actually learned?

00:09:02.799 --> 00:09:05.830
And we developed a taxonomy for this

00:09:05.830 --> 00:09:08.395
as far as numbers of
objects, scale of objects,

00:09:08.395 --> 00:09:11.020
types of application.

00:09:11.020 --> 00:09:13.070
I won't bore you with the whole thing

00:09:13.070 --> 00:09:18.070
but most of it is either
improving legibility

00:09:18.680 --> 00:09:22.680
or doing restoration is what
people have done so far.

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:25.667
So, making so tablets, caneiform

00:09:25.667 --> 00:09:27.793
and so forth easier to read.

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:30.700
Reassembling broken pieces,

00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:33.410
this is from Benedict Browns work when

00:09:33.410 --> 00:09:34.680
back when he was at Princeton

00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:38.303
but putting together
in this case Frescoes.

00:09:39.970 --> 00:09:43.410
There's another case
of Irish tax documents

00:09:43.410 --> 00:09:47.630
that were damaged in
the fire and that were

00:09:47.630 --> 00:09:51.360
we have scanned with photogrammetry

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:54.093
so that they can be
digitally flattened and read.

00:09:56.510 --> 00:09:58.580
This is from University of Kentucky

00:09:58.580 --> 00:10:03.580
where they do CAT scan
and volumetric scanning

00:10:03.920 --> 00:10:07.060
of fragile documents so
that you can virtually

00:10:07.060 --> 00:10:08.543
unroll and read them.

00:10:09.830 --> 00:10:11.970
And then there's a lot
of projects which barrel

00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:14.449
from fairly standard engineering

00:10:14.449 --> 00:10:19.280
and other aerial survey techniques

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:22.721
to do things like monitoring
of historic structures

00:10:22.721 --> 00:10:25.730
or in this case the same sort of thing

00:10:25.730 --> 00:10:30.190
you might do for like crop
management or enemy surveillance

00:10:30.190 --> 00:10:34.570
using aerial data to
understand usage patterns.

00:10:34.570 --> 00:10:39.130
So, a lot of work that involves
basically a single object.

00:10:39.130 --> 00:10:41.909
We haven't seen much with collections.

00:10:41.909 --> 00:10:45.050
This is a little bit of work on that line,

00:10:45.050 --> 00:10:47.120
we're looking at sets of coins

00:10:47.120 --> 00:10:51.560
and finding the same
figure across several coins

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:52.623
that were found.

00:10:53.480 --> 00:10:57.993
This is another studying the faces

00:10:57.993 --> 00:11:02.993
on the Banyan temple in Cambodia

00:11:03.476 --> 00:11:05.824
with the goal immediately,

00:11:05.824 --> 00:11:10.110
the immediate goal of
trying to do restorations.

00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:12.700
This is one true collection study

00:11:12.700 --> 00:11:16.900
where these are figures
3D scanned at the Louvre

00:11:16.900 --> 00:11:21.900
to try to determine provenance
for pieces that you know

00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:27.443
that the their origins aren't known.

00:11:29.290 --> 00:11:33.330
And this is a study of
a smaller collection

00:11:33.330 --> 00:11:38.330
of busts of Augustus that
were done to try to find

00:11:39.499 --> 00:11:42.613
the relationship of when when the busts

00:11:42.613 --> 00:11:44.423
were done and by whom.

00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:50.273
But overall in our whole survey taxonomy

00:11:50.273 --> 00:11:55.273
the collection analysis which
it's just like the big thing

00:11:55.760 --> 00:11:57.990
we can do but you know
to do things at scale,

00:11:57.990 --> 00:12:01.070
it's a very empty space right now.

00:12:01.070 --> 00:12:02.130
So, what is the problem?

00:12:02.130 --> 00:12:05.445
Why aren't we doing more
about bringing objects

00:12:05.445 --> 00:12:07.790
from different museums together

00:12:07.790 --> 00:12:10.690
and doing these collection analysis?

00:12:10.690 --> 00:12:13.810
And I think there's two
things that we have a lot of

00:12:13.810 --> 00:12:18.750
very small collections that
are scattered geographically

00:12:18.750 --> 00:12:21.960
and they are often lacking contextual data

00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:23.690
that would make them useful.

00:12:23.690 --> 00:12:26.910
I mean this virtual curation
lab which shows up on Sketchfab

00:12:26.910 --> 00:12:29.900
and if you're not like sort
of a computer game builder

00:12:29.900 --> 00:12:31.900
you probably don't use this site much

00:12:31.900 --> 00:12:34.730
but it's where people park
a lot of computer models

00:12:34.730 --> 00:12:36.540
because you can.

00:12:36.540 --> 00:12:39.240
And it takes a lot of
effort to build this site

00:12:39.240 --> 00:12:42.548
that this site Virginia
Commonwealth University

00:12:42.548 --> 00:12:45.020
but the models they are from the most part

00:12:45.020 --> 00:12:48.400
they are simplified from what
they were originally scanned

00:12:48.400 --> 00:12:49.970
because you know just so that

00:12:49.970 --> 00:12:52.210
they can be displayable at home

00:12:52.210 --> 00:12:55.120
and there's very little contextual data.

00:12:55.120 --> 00:12:58.080
And it's it's kind of a
smattering of stuff from all over.

00:12:58.080 --> 00:13:00.920
It's not apples to apples kinds of studies

00:13:00.920 --> 00:13:02.373
that we want to do.

00:13:03.650 --> 00:13:08.360
CyArk which for many years has
been going around the world

00:13:08.360 --> 00:13:11.010
doing large-scale scanning of structures

00:13:11.010 --> 00:13:13.550
has made all of their data open now.

00:13:13.550 --> 00:13:16.220
So, there's dozens and dozens of sites

00:13:16.220 --> 00:13:19.508
ranging from huts in
Antarctica to cathedrals

00:13:19.508 --> 00:13:22.060
to Cape Canaveral.

00:13:22.060 --> 00:13:25.520
So, a lot of diverse places and there's no

00:13:25.520 --> 00:13:27.240
lack of quantity of data,

00:13:27.240 --> 00:13:29.670
there's gigabytes and
gigabytes and gigabytes.

00:13:29.670 --> 00:13:32.203
But you know it's still like

00:13:32.203 --> 00:13:33.280
it's not clear.

00:13:33.280 --> 00:13:34.460
How can we make use of this?

00:13:34.460 --> 00:13:35.293
What do we need?

00:13:35.293 --> 00:13:37.710
Is it just managing the sheer volume?

00:13:37.710 --> 00:13:41.320
I think we also need contextual
data that goes with it

00:13:41.320 --> 00:13:43.100
to understand what we're trying to study,

00:13:43.100 --> 00:13:44.793
what questions we're answering.

00:13:47.290 --> 00:13:51.590
Just shifting then from
3D to 2D where obviously

00:13:51.590 --> 00:13:53.030
there's been a lot more success

00:13:53.030 --> 00:13:54.846
because it's one less D.

00:13:54.846 --> 00:13:58.290
So, like for example we've had work here

00:13:58.290 --> 00:14:01.680
in studying medieval manuscripts

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:03.970
where a lot of work has been you know

00:14:03.970 --> 00:14:06.120
it's the same thing
there's many manuscripts

00:14:06.120 --> 00:14:07.440
in many different places

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:10.410
so viewers have been built
that can take you know

00:14:10.410 --> 00:14:12.784
and standard representations

00:14:12.784 --> 00:14:15.410
so that you can take the manuscript pages

00:14:15.410 --> 00:14:18.650
from many different sources
and read and compare them.

00:14:18.650 --> 00:14:20.740
This is work from mainly from Stanford

00:14:20.740 --> 00:14:22.433
in collaboration with Yale.

00:14:22.433 --> 00:14:26.530
On our side we did some various test cases

00:14:26.530 --> 00:14:30.960
to illustrate studying
large quantities of pages,

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:35.960
both their layout and
their spectral reflectance.

00:14:37.470 --> 00:14:42.260
The layout is of interest to
identify things ahead of time

00:14:42.260 --> 00:14:44.360
sort of adding data to things

00:14:44.360 --> 00:14:47.670
so that's subsequently when
people wanna ask questions

00:14:47.670 --> 00:14:50.180
it's easy to access the results.

00:14:50.180 --> 00:14:53.460
And so it's layout not
so much the the words,

00:14:53.460 --> 00:14:56.210
I mean these are from Books of Ours.

00:14:56.210 --> 00:14:57.170
I mean they're prayer books,

00:14:57.170 --> 00:14:58.360
everybody knows what they said.

00:14:58.360 --> 00:15:00.310
That was kind of the point of them.

00:15:00.310 --> 00:15:02.240
But what's interesting
is the different ways

00:15:02.240 --> 00:15:05.840
that they are laid out and
spotting things like these.

00:15:05.840 --> 00:15:07.460
I don't have a arrow either here.

00:15:07.460 --> 00:15:11.950
These little bars that
indicate that this is poetry

00:15:11.950 --> 00:15:14.543
as opposed to the the main text.

00:15:15.380 --> 00:15:19.880
So, we did a lot of work you
know consistently identifying

00:15:19.880 --> 00:15:23.720
layout across and the
challenges in these manuscripts

00:15:23.720 --> 00:15:26.240
being lots of damage and things missing

00:15:26.240 --> 00:15:29.101
and variety of that sort of thing.

00:15:29.101 --> 00:15:34.101
We also did a study of the
apparent color of pigments

00:15:36.280 --> 00:15:38.935
to understand changes that may indicate

00:15:38.935 --> 00:15:43.130
things being made by different
people in different places.

00:15:43.130 --> 00:15:45.560
We did this doing hyperspectral imaging,

00:15:45.560 --> 00:15:47.020
I won't go into,

00:15:47.020 --> 00:15:49.772
as opposed to regular RGB cameras.

00:15:49.772 --> 00:15:52.580
And what was interesting
there's like, oh great!

00:15:52.580 --> 00:15:55.040
These things look
different and now not only

00:15:55.040 --> 00:15:57.590
do they look different
I have different plots

00:15:57.590 --> 00:15:58.847
that are associated with them

00:15:58.847 --> 00:16:01.194
and it's still like and so what.

00:16:01.194 --> 00:16:03.380
So, is that a significant difference

00:16:03.380 --> 00:16:05.490
or not a significant difference?

00:16:05.490 --> 00:16:08.700
So, we go on with the
analysis to do things like

00:16:08.700 --> 00:16:12.100
go through thousands of
pages and cluster the colors

00:16:12.100 --> 00:16:13.700
to count the colors and then see

00:16:13.700 --> 00:16:16.820
what is the spectral
variation within pages

00:16:16.820 --> 00:16:20.150
to understand what's a normal distribution

00:16:20.150 --> 00:16:24.030
of color variations as
opposed to something that's

00:16:24.030 --> 00:16:28.023
indicates some sort of change
in authorship or manufacturer.

00:16:31.530 --> 00:16:33.820
Another sort of long-term
thing that's bugged me,

00:16:33.820 --> 00:16:35.520
I haven't made much progress on,

00:16:35.520 --> 00:16:37.580
is you know like the
Google arts and culture

00:16:37.580 --> 00:16:41.230
they have thousands and thousands
of high-resolution images.

00:16:41.230 --> 00:16:42.380
Like what can we do with them?

00:16:42.380 --> 00:16:43.960
What is anybody doing with them?

00:16:43.960 --> 00:16:47.560
What is, so one thing is
studying crack patterns

00:16:47.560 --> 00:16:51.150
in the paintings to understand
where the cracks came from,

00:16:51.150 --> 00:16:52.710
what can we do about them

00:16:52.710 --> 00:16:56.680
and if you're lucky and and
on Rembrandt's face or his hat

00:16:56.680 --> 00:16:59.080
you have light paint, you
can you can sort of see them.

00:16:59.080 --> 00:17:01.988
Although even here
extracting them reliably

00:17:01.988 --> 00:17:05.600
with image processing is difficult

00:17:05.600 --> 00:17:07.810
and so I've never really
had had various students

00:17:07.810 --> 00:17:09.683
start on this and get frustrated.

00:17:10.630 --> 00:17:15.427
A brilliant idea that
Lukasz brought us who,

00:17:15.427 --> 00:17:20.427
not him, who recently was at the Yale IPCH

00:17:20.930 --> 00:17:25.175
and is now running a an
analysis lab in Poland

00:17:25.175 --> 00:17:28.810
working with the Yale
Center for British Art

00:17:28.810 --> 00:17:33.100
is this idea of using x-rays of the images

00:17:33.100 --> 00:17:36.620
rather than just trying to
see the cracks on the images.

00:17:36.620 --> 00:17:39.290
So there's an x-ray and you
have to see it close-up.

00:17:39.290 --> 00:17:41.670
Okay, the cracks are harder to see

00:17:41.670 --> 00:17:43.750
but there's a lot less in the way of them

00:17:43.750 --> 00:17:46.150
as far as dark paint, light paint.

00:17:46.150 --> 00:17:51.150
So, we have a chance to extract
the cracks from the X ray

00:17:51.640 --> 00:17:54.790
and then learn using deep learning

00:17:54.790 --> 00:17:56.470
the relationship between those cracks

00:17:56.470 --> 00:17:58.120
and the appearance of the paintings.

00:17:58.120 --> 00:18:00.150
So, that then once we learned that

00:18:00.150 --> 00:18:02.820
we can probably at least to some extent

00:18:02.820 --> 00:18:04.480
detect cracks in paintings

00:18:04.480 --> 00:18:06.770
that we don't have the x-rays for.

00:18:06.770 --> 00:18:09.750
It's not a slam-dunk,
there's a lot of work to do

00:18:09.750 --> 00:18:13.373
but it's it's a great idea
that we'll be pursuing.

00:18:15.069 --> 00:18:18.351
On the last topic I wanted to touch on

00:18:18.351 --> 00:18:20.250
which it was already mentioned

00:18:20.250 --> 00:18:22.330
is this problem of heterogeneous data.

00:18:22.330 --> 00:18:25.500
You've got all sorts of information

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:28.130
and this is similar to I think
the last couple of projects

00:18:28.130 --> 00:18:28.970
you were talking about

00:18:28.970 --> 00:18:31.350
so I think we may have
some overlap in tools

00:18:31.350 --> 00:18:33.950
we might want to develop.

00:18:33.950 --> 00:18:37.210
This is Elihu Rubin from
the School of Architecture

00:18:37.210 --> 00:18:40.599
has developed this over many years

00:18:40.599 --> 00:18:43.110
through a course he teaches

00:18:43.110 --> 00:18:47.960
where students document details
of buildings in New Haven.

00:18:47.960 --> 00:18:49.970
The idea is like history's not just

00:18:49.970 --> 00:18:52.850
the great persons, the famous buildings

00:18:52.850 --> 00:18:56.409
but it's our communities,
it's where we live and our

00:18:56.409 --> 00:18:58.730
where we live is is New Haven.

00:18:58.730 --> 00:19:02.520
So, documenting the individual
buildings in New Haven.

00:19:02.520 --> 00:19:05.080
This is something that
Sima Octa who's here

00:19:05.080 --> 00:19:08.413
has been working on as
well as as Benedict.

00:19:08.413 --> 00:19:13.350
So, in this archive each
students responsible

00:19:13.350 --> 00:19:14.940
for finding as much as they can.

00:19:14.940 --> 00:19:18.430
That includes maps from the library.

00:19:18.430 --> 00:19:23.430
It includes scans of permit applications

00:19:23.593 --> 00:19:25.680
for changes in the building,

00:19:25.680 --> 00:19:28.550
lists of who occupied it through time,

00:19:28.550 --> 00:19:32.440
just every every little
you know scrap of paper

00:19:32.440 --> 00:19:34.950
associated with transactions,

00:19:34.950 --> 00:19:38.410
historic photos, stories, social history

00:19:38.410 --> 00:19:43.190
so that we're getting this
database of New Haven.

00:19:43.190 --> 00:19:44.270
So, then the question is,

00:19:44.270 --> 00:19:46.780
now we're getting this great
heterogeneous database,

00:19:46.780 --> 00:19:48.450
what kind of studies can we do?

00:19:48.450 --> 00:19:50.870
And what tools would
be needed to go across

00:19:50.870 --> 00:19:53.913
this corpus of images and texts?

00:19:55.240 --> 00:19:57.610
So, that's what I wanted to bring up

00:19:57.610 --> 00:20:01.450
was sort of these ideas
like why aren't we using 3D?

00:20:01.450 --> 00:20:03.310
We need context.

00:20:03.310 --> 00:20:04.966
Where did the data come from?

00:20:04.966 --> 00:20:07.270
And more things like you know,

00:20:07.270 --> 00:20:10.060
what was the data, the
postcard and so forth?

00:20:10.060 --> 00:20:15.060
And 2D this enhancing the
data with new correlations

00:20:16.547 --> 00:20:20.240
from labeled samples
and just our new tools

00:20:20.240 --> 00:20:23.233
for learning from heterogeneous data.

00:20:24.198 --> 00:20:25.365
So, thank you.

00:20:26.412 --> 00:20:29.412
(audience applauds)

